  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Addressing decent work deficits in the tobacco sector of Tanzania</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The promotion of decent work in Tanzania faces challenges such as poor working conditions, unsafe environments, and the prevalence of child labour, particularly in rural sectors like agriculture and the informal economy. The ILO aims to strengthen labour standards, sectoral guidelines, and occupational safety, while fostering social dialogue to address these issues. This intervention will focus on enhancing the capacity of Tanzania&apos;s government and social partners to implement and promote international labour standards and sector-specific codes of practice, with a particular emphasis on agriculture, the cultural and creative economy, and other emerging sectors.

During the biennium, the ILO will support the development of a national roadmap to promote decent work in Tanzania&#x2019;s key sectors, including the Culture and Creative Economy (CCE), and facilitate the adoption of tools to improve compliance with labour laws. A focus will be placed on improving occupational safety and health (OSH) frameworks, particularly for workers in hazardous environments like agriculture.

Key beneficiaries include workers in high-risk sectors, such as agriculture and CCE, as well as government institutions, employers&apos; and workers&apos; organizations, and labour inspectors. These groups will benefit from capacity-building, technical assistance, and policy development aimed at improving the overall quality of work and social protection systems.

The strategy will be implemented in collaboration with government authorities, social partners, and other relevant stakeholders. The expected outcomes include strengthened institutional capacity for promoting decent work, improved enforcement of labour laws, and the enhancement of social dialogue to ensure better working conditions across sectors.</narrative>
  </description>
